   “It’s an instrument.” She clicked once, at nothing, to test it.
   I ran sound, which is gig-adjacent: Mr. Rodríguez doesn’t pay me, he
feeds me. I had the contact mic puck on the steel edge of the counter,
where the El’s rumble came up through the metal like a bass line, and
the projector aimed at the brick of the back wall. The Garden fed on
everything. Every cup that clinked, every chair that scraped, every
laugh turned into color that spread across the brick like spilled ink
finding its level. The room liked it. Somebody whooped.
   A girl named Marisol, sixteen, read a poem about her father’s hands,
cracked from the car wash, and halfway through the room forgot to
breathe. A viejo nobody had ever seen before played a bachata on a
nylon-string guitar so softly that the espresso machine felt guilty. A kid
from Tremont rapped about his block with a precision that made the
older heads do the slow nod, the one that means remember that name.
Mr. Rodríguez’s friend Teodoro read a twelve-minute poem about a
parking ticket. It had a second act.
   At 8:40, Teodoro’s brother-in-law said “Get to the ticket,” out loud.
Sofía clicked and wrote: raised voice (provoked).

   Junior: bodega 4. a lady threw a shoe
   Sofía (to me, across the room): cafe 1. shoe lady not applicable

Yari, who works nights and treats the rest of the world as a time zone
she’s visiting, texted at 8:55.

   Yari: sofia says your wall is doing a thing. send video. i’m on hour nine and a man
